Intuition
The array is sorted but rotated at some pivot. So, the entire array is not sorted, but at least one half of the array will always be sorted.

We can use Binary Search to efficiently find the target by identifying which half is sorted and checking whether the target lies within that half.

Approach
Find the middle index using si + (ei - si) / 2.
If nums[mid] == target, return mid.
Check whether the left half is sorted using nums[si] <= nums[mid].
If the left half is sorted:
Check whether the target lies between nums[si] and nums[mid].
If yes, search in the left half.
Otherwise, search in the right half.
If the right half is sorted:
Check whether the target lies between nums[mid] and nums[ei].
If yes, search in the right half.
Otherwise, search in the left half.
Repeat recursively until the target is found or si > ei, in which case return -1.
Complexity
Time complexity: (O(log n)) — We eliminate half of the search space in every recursive call.
Space complexity: (O(log n)) — Due to the recursive call stack.
Code
class Solution {
    public int search(int[] nums, int target) {
        return helper(nums, target, 0, nums.length - 1);
    }

    public int helper(int[] nums, int target, int si, int ei) {
        if (si > ei) {
            return -1;
        }

        int mid = si + (ei - si) / 2;

        if (nums[mid] == target) {
            return mid;
        }

        if (nums[si] <= nums[mid]) {
            // Left half is sorted
            if (nums[si] <= target && target <= nums[mid]) {
                return helper(nums, target, si, mid - 1);
            } else {
                return helper(nums, target, mid + 1, ei);
            }
        } else {
            // Right half is sorted
            if (nums[mid] <= target && target <= nums[ei]) {
                return helper(nums, target, mid + 1, ei);
            } else {
                return helper(nums, target, si, mid - 1);
            }
        }
    }
}
 
